2. What we did
A. Investigated the original training failure

The original collection contained 289 payments, but only four independent fraud groups.
All fraud examples landed in training, leaving validation with only normal transactions.
We retained grouped separation and collected additional independent synthetic scenarios instead of searching random seeds for a convenient split.

B. Trained and evaluated v2

Collected 300 payments, all matched to archived decision snapshots.
Trained v2 with scenario-aware grouped splits.
Verified exported JSON predictions matched scikit-learn.
V2 improved the original synthetic test recall.
A separate challenge dataset exposed a regression: all five legitimate new-device examples were flagged.
V2 promotion was blocked.

C. Collected richer data and trained v3

Collected 444 payments across 96 independent scenario groups.
Included legitimate new-device transactions and fraud using trusted devices.
Preserved subtype/device-treatment coverage across splits.
Trained v3 with 296 training, 74 validation, and 74 test payments.
V3 flagged 52/58 test fraud payments, compared with v1’s 33/58.
Neither flagged the 16 normal test payments.
V3 avoided the known v2 new-device regression.
These remain limited, fraud-heavy synthetic results—not production qualification.

D. Added candidate-only shadow scoring

Kept v1 responsible for actual decisions.
Added v3 scores, identity, checksum, simulated decisions, status, and inference duration to published decision events.
Preserved the active API result.
Deployed an isolated canary on 8081, outside the original HAProxy path.
Verified one request end-to-end through S3, then a 12-request batch.

E. Implemented candidate S3 hot reload

Created checksum-addressed model releases and a separate shadow manifest.
Implemented bounded downloads, checksum/version validation, background polling, and last-known-good retention.
Verified a real S3 artifact download.
Switched to an identity-only candidate test version and restored the original.
Added worker identities.
Verified both same workers observed replacement and restoration without restarting.
Ran the 150-request continuity test successfully.

F. Prepared promotion evidence

Created a local checksum-bound review record.
Bound it to model, baseline, dataset, configuration, and evaluation evidence.
Left review status pending and promotion false.

G. Implemented active-model loading

Added a separate active manifest contract.
Added immutable snapshots and checksum authorization.
Captured one model snapshot per request for scoring and event identity.
Tested v1 scoring parity, invalid-artifact rejection, concurrent refresh behavior, and shutdown.
Published the original v1 release.
Enabled active polling on the canary.
Verified both workers used the exact original v1 checksum.
Prepared an identical-coefficient v1-reload-test artifact.
Added optional authorization for its checksum.
Verified replacement and rollback offline inside the built image.
Uploaded that test artifact and enabled its authorization on the canary.
Real active-pointer switching and rollback remain outstanding.

H. Started the SageMaker data and training foundation

Added UTC-partitioned decision serialization.
Added backward-compatible partitioned and legacy writes.
Implemented as-of feedback resolution.
Implemented archived-feature/label joins with maturity and cutoff checks.
Added checksum-bound processing bundles.
Added chronological train/validation/test preparation with separate label-availability cutoffs.
Added SageMaker-compatible training and baseline-comparison evaluation.
Created the dedicated execution role.
Built a separate batch image and passed all 10 training/evaluation tests inside it.


